items/lem-limit-of-tail.md:56:[L1] Convergence and the Cauchy condition: $(z_k)$ converges to $z$ when for every rational $\varepsilon > 0$ there is $N$ with $|z_k - z| < \varepsilon$ for all $k \ge N$, and $(z_k)$ is Cauchy when for every rational $\varepsilon > 0$ there is $N$ with $|z_k - z_l| < \varepsilon$ for all $k, l \ge N$ ([[def-real-limit]]).
items/lem-limit-of-tail.md:58:[L2] Index arithmetic, first half: $j \ge N$ implies $j + K \ge N + K$, and $N + K \ge N$ ([[def-nat-order]], [[lem-nat-order-add-compatible]]).
items/lem-limit-of-tail.md:60:[L3] Index arithmetic, second half: every $k \ge N + K$ has the form $k = j + K$ with $j \ge N$. Indeed $N + K \le k$ gives $i$ with $(N+K) + i = k$; put $j := N + i$, so that $j \ge N$ and $j + K = (N+i) + K = N + (i + K) = N + (K + i) = (N+K) + i = k$ ([[def-nat-order]], [[lem-nat-add-associative]], [[lem-nat-add-commutative]]).
items/lem-limit-of-tail.md:62:[L4] The order on $\mathbb{N}$ is reflexive and transitive, so $k \ge N + K \ge N$ gives $k \ge N$; and $j + 0 = j$ for every $j$ ([[thm-nat-linear-order]], [[def-nat-order]], [[def-nat-addition]]).
items/lem-convergent-implies-bounded.md:47:[L1] Convergence: for every rational $\varepsilon > 0$ there is $K \in \mathbb{N}$ with $|x_k - x| < \varepsilon$ for all $k \ge K$ ([[def-real-limit]]); and $1$ is a rational $> 0$, since $\mathbb{Q}$ is an ordered field ([[thm-rat-ordered-field]]) and $0 < 1$ in every ordered field ([[cor-of-one-positive]]).
items/lem-convergent-implies-bounded.md:49:[L2] Triangle inequality: $|a + b| \le |a| + |b|$ in $\mathbb{R}$ ([[lem-of-triangle-inequality]], [[def-complete-ordered-field]]).
items/lem-convergent-implies-bounded.md:51:[L3] Absolute value: $|u| \ge 0$ for every real $u$ ([[lem-of-abs-value]]).
items/lem-convergent-implies-bounded.md:53:[L4] Finite maxima: for every $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and all reals $a_0, \dots, a_n$ the set $\{a_0, \dots, a_n\}$ has a maximum ([[lem-finite-set-has-max]]); a maximum lies in the set and dominates every element of it ([[def-max-min]]).
items/lem-convergent-implies-bounded.md:55:[L5] Order arithmetic in $\mathbb{R}$: transitivity of $\le$ and of $<$; both mixed forms, $u < v \le w \Rightarrow u < w$ and $u \le v < w \Rightarrow u < w$, and the implication $u < v \Rightarrow u \le v$, all immediate from the reading of $u \le v$ as "$u < v$ or $u = v$" ([[def-complete-ordered-field]], [[def-ordered-field]]); and translation invariance, $a < b \Rightarrow a + c < b + c$ ([[lem-of-add-order]]).
items/lem-convergent-implies-bounded.md:57:[L6] The order on $\mathbb{N}$ is total, so every index $k$ satisfies $k < K$ or $k \ge K$ ([[thm-nat-linear-order]]).
items/lem-convergent-implies-bounded.md:63:1.1 Apply convergence with the rational $\varepsilon = 1$: fix $K \in \mathbb{N}$ such that $|x_k - x| < 1$ for all $k \ge K$. [L1]
items/lem-convergent-implies-bounded.md:67:2.2 Define $M := \max\{\, |x_0|, |x_1|, \dots, |x_{K-1}|, |x| + 1 \,\}$, the maximum of the finite list obtained by appending $|x| + 1$ to the first $K$ values $|x_0|, \dots, |x_{K-1}|$; the list is nonempty because its last entry is always present (when $K = 0$ the list is the single entry $|x| + 1$), so the maximum exists by [L4]. [step 1.1, L4, choose]
## Proof

**Proof technique:** direct.

1.1 (1) implies (2). Assume $(x_k)$ converges to $x$, let $K \in \mathbb{N}$ be arbitrary and let $\varepsilon > 0$ be rational; take $N$ with $|x_k - x| < \varepsilon$ for all $k \ge N$. For every $j \ge N$ we have $j + K \ge N + K \ge N$, hence $|x^{(K)}_j - x| = |x_{j+K} - x| < \varepsilon$; so $x^{(K)}$ converges to $x$. [assume-hyp, L1, L2, L4]

1.2 (2) implies (3), in both families. The $0$-th tail satisfies $x^{(0)}_j = x_{j+0} = x_j$, so $(x_k)$ is itself one of its own tails; statement (2) quantifies over all tails, so applying it to this one already yields (3). [given, L4]

1.3 (3) implies (1). Assume $x^{(K)}$ converges to $x$ for some fixed $K$, and let $\varepsilon > 0$ be rational; take $N$ with $|x^{(K)}_j - x| < \varepsilon$ for all $j \ge N$. Every $k \ge N + K$ is of the form $j + K$ with $j \ge N$, so $|x_k - x| = |x^{(K)}_j - x| < \varepsilon$; taking $N + K$ as the threshold shows $(x_k)$ converges to $x$. [assume-hyp, L1, L3]

1.4 The Cauchy version of (1) implies (2). Assume $(x_k)$ is Cauchy, fix $K$ and a rational $\varepsilon > 0$, and take $N$ with $|x_k - x_l| < \varepsilon$ for all $k, l \ge N$. For $i, j \ge N$ both $i + K \ge N$ and $j + K \ge N$, so $|x^{(K)}_i - x^{(K)}_j| = |x_{i+K} - x_{j+K}| < \varepsilon$; so $x^{(K)}$ is Cauchy. [assume-hyp, L1, L2, L4]

1.5 The Cauchy version of (3) implies (1). Assume $x^{(K)}$ is Cauchy and let $\varepsilon > 0$ be rational; take $N$ with $|x^{(K)}_i - x^{(K)}_j| < \varepsilon$ for all $i, j \ge N$. Any $k, l \ge N + K$ are of the form $i + K$ and $j + K$ with $i, j \ge N$, so $|x_k - x_l| < \varepsilon$; so $(x_k)$ is Cauchy. [assume-hyp, L1, L3]

2.1 In each of the two families the cycle (1) implies (2) implies (3) implies (1) is closed, so within each family the three statements are equivalent. [step 1.1, step 1.2, step 1.3, step 1.4, step 1.5]

3.1 If $(x_k)$ and $(y_k)$ agree from index $K$ on, then $x^{(K)} = y^{(K)}$ as functions, so by the established equivalence $(x_k)$ converges to $x$ exactly when $y^{(K)}$ does, exactly when $(y_k)$ does, and the same chain applies to the Cauchy condition. [step 2.1, given] ∎

