42:([[def-sequence]], [[def-real-limit]]), and let $n : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}$
50:**Given:** A sequence $(x_k)$ of reals converging to a real $x$, and a strictly increasing $n : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}$, so that $(x_{n_k})$ is a subsequence of $(x_k)$ ([[def-sequence]], [[def-real-limit]]).
52:[L1] Convergence, quantified over rational $\varepsilon > 0$ ([[def-real-limit]]).
54:[L2] Growth of the index map: $n_k \ge k$ for every $k$ ([[lem-index-map-grows]]).
56:[L3] The order on $\mathbb{N}$ is transitive, so $n_k \ge k \ge K$ gives $n_k \ge K$ ([[thm-nat-linear-order]], [[def-nat-order]]).
58:[L4] A sequence has at most one limit ([[lem-limit-unique]]).
76:- The converse of the first claim is false: **one** convergent subsequence says nothing about the sequence ([[fs-subsequence-convergence-implies-convergence]]). It is the divergence test, not the convergence of a single subsequence, that is usable.
78:- The divergence test is the standard way to show a concrete sequence diverges, and it is how [[fs-bounded-implies-convergent]] is refuted. The opposite direction, extracting a convergent subsequence from a bounded sequence, is Bolzano-Weierstrass; it is **not proved anywhere in this library as things stand**, being the subject of the next page of this track, planned and not yet written.
