+{"id":"def-sequence","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/ch3.pdf","https://www.math.cmu.edu/~gautam/c/2026-269/notes/compactness.html"],"rationale":"Hunter Definition 3.8 gives a real sequence as a function N to R, Definition 3.17 gives boundedness, Definition 3.21 gives the eventual/cofinal vocabulary, and the CMU notes define subsequences by composition with a strictly increasing index sequence. The item is a standard bundle with 0-based indexing, a range clause, tails, and the library's boundedness convention, so it is a semantic consolidation rather than an exact transcription.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
+{"id":"lem-limit-of-tail","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/ch3.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter states that shifted tails have the same convergence properties and limits and that finite changes do not affect convergence (Chapter 3, after Proposition 3.19). The item expands this standard tail invariance into a three-way equivalence for convergence and Cauchyness and adds eventual equality. The detailed index-arithmetic proof is a local construction rather than a recoverable sourced proof.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
+{"id":"lem-limit-unique","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/ch3.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter Proposition 3.11 states exactly that a convergent real sequence has a unique limit. The local proof adapts Hunter's epsilon/2 triangle-inequality contradiction to the library's rational-epsilon convention and ordered-field dependency interface.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
+{"id":"lem-convergent-implies-bounded","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"literature-derived","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/ch3.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter Proposition 3.19 states exactly that every convergent real sequence is bounded, and its proof uses epsilon=1, the triangle inequality, and a maximum over the finitely many initial terms, the same argument used here with the harmless 0-based indexing adjustment.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
+{"id":"lem-convergent-implies-cauchy","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"literature-derived","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/ch3.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter Theorem 3.46 states convergence if and only if Cauchyness and proves the forward direction by the same epsilon/2 triangle-inequality argument. The item isolates only that direction and uses rational epsilon, so its Statement is a faithful specialization rather than the source theorem verbatim.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
+{"id":"lem-null-times-bounded","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.ms.uky.edu/~lee/ma502/notes7/node3.html","https://people.reed.edu/~mayer/math112.html/html2/node12.html"],"rationale":"The Kentucky sequence-limit notes state exactly that the product of a bounded sequence and a null sequence is null; Reed's notes label the same result the null-times-bounded theorem. The local proof, including the M+1 device and real-to-rational epsilon bridge, is independently written.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
+{"id":"thm-algebra-of-limits","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-2/pages/5-1-sequences","https://www.nku.edu/~longa/classes/2011fall/mat420/days/highlights/highlights2.1.pdf"],"rationale":"OpenStax and the NKU notes give the standard sum, scalar, product, reciprocal, and quotient limit laws with the nonzero hypotheses. The item packages those laws together, adds difference explicitly, and states an eventual denominator lower bound; its proof adapts the standard null-sequence and reverse-triangle arguments to the library interface.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
+{"id":"lem-limit-preserves-order","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/ch3.pdf","https://www.nku.edu/~longa/classes/2011fall/mat420/days/highlights/highlights2.1.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter Theorem 3.22 and NKU Theorem 2-5 give preservation of non-strict inequalities by limits. The item allows the comparison only eventually and appends constant-sequence special cases. Its proof is an adapted one-sequence contradiction through y_k-x_k.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
+{"id":"thm-squeeze","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"literature-derived","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/ch3.pdf","https://www.nku.edu/~longa/classes/2011fall/mat420/days/highlights/highlights2.1.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter Theorem 3.23 and NKU Theorem 2-5(iv) state the squeeze theorem with pointwise bracketing. The item makes the standard finite-tail strengthening from pointwise to eventual bracketing. Its epsilon proof follows the sourced sandwich proof with a common threshold.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
+{"id":"lem-limit-abs","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/ch3.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter Corollary 3.25 states that x_n to x implies |x_n| to |x| and proves it by the reverse triangle inequality. The item adds the reverse implication at x=0 and the precise warning that the reverse fails away from zero, so it is a standard result with an added boundary clause.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
+{"id":"def-divergence-to-infinity","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/ch3.pdf","https://www.nku.edu/~longa/classes/2011fall/mat420/days/highlights/highlights2.1.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter Definition 3.12 and the NKU notes give the same quantified definition of divergence to positive and negative infinity and explicitly distinguish it from convergence. The library uses 0-based indices, a weak threshold k>=K instead of n>N, and adds unboundedness/convergence consequences, so the construction is semantically identical but not textually exact.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
+{"id":"lem-reciprocal-of-null-diverges","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.nku.edu/~longa/classes/2011fall/mat420/days/highlights/highlights2.1.pdf","https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/ch3.pdf"],"rationale":"The NKU notes place the positive null sequence 1/n and its reciprocal n side by side under the definitions of convergence to zero and divergence to infinity; Hunter gives the same definitions and examples. The item abstracts that standard reciprocal bridge into an iff for arbitrary positive sequences. The two-direction epsilon/M proof is locally composed.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
+{"id":"lem-index-map-grows","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.cuhk.edu.hk/course_builder/2324/math2050b/MATH%202050%20L10%20-%20Subsequences%20and%20Bolzano-Weierstrass%20Theorem.pdf","https://www.math.cmu.edu/~gautam/c/2026-269/notes/compactness.html"],"rationale":"The CUHK analysis notes state n_k>=k for every strictly increasing subsequence index map and prove it by induction; the CMU notes use the same strictly increasing index-map convention. The item also proves that consecutive strict comparisons imply global strict increase and shifts to 0-based indexing, so it is a semantic expansion of the standard index lemma.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
+{"id":"lem-subsequence-inherits-limit","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/ch3.pdf","https://www.math.cuhk.edu.hk/course_builder/2324/math2050b/MATH%202050%20L10%20-%20Subsequences%20and%20Bolzano-Weierstrass%20Theorem.pdf","https://www.math.cmu.edu/~gautam/c/2026-269/notes/compactness.html"],"rationale":"Hunter Proposition 3.50, the CUHK theorem, and CMU Proposition 4 state that every subsequence of a convergent sequence has the same limit; Hunter's following corollary supplies the distinct-subsequential-limits divergence test. The item combines both standard propositions and uses the 0-based n_k>=k formulation.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
+{"id":"rem-sequence-conventions","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/ch3.pdf","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Limit_of_a_sequence","https://www.math.cmu.edu/~gautam/c/2026-269/notes/compactness.html"],"rationale":"The sources collectively support the standard indexing, tail invariance, eventuality, uniqueness-before-limit-notation, bounded-range, divergence, rational/real epsilon, and subsequence conventions. The item is a library-specific synthesis with reading-order and dependency-routing commentary, not a novel mathematical claim.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
+{"id":"fs-bounded-implies-convergent","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/ch3.pdf","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Limit_of_a_sequence"],"rationale":"Hunter explicitly says boundedness is not sufficient for convergence and uses the alternating 1,-1 sequence as the counterexample; the same standard counterexample appears in the limit literature. The item recasts it through recursion and two explicit subsequence index maps, so the Statement construction is altered and the detailed refutation is locally generated.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
+{"id":"fs-limit-preserves-strict-inequality","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/ch3.pdf","https://www.nku.edu/~longa/classes/2011fall/mat420/days/highlights/highlights2.1.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter's order theorem preserves only non-strict inequalities and its standard 1/n example converges through positive terms to zero; the NKU notes give the same 1/n limit and non-strict order theorem. The item packages these as the explicit false reversal and supplies an Archimedean epsilon proof, so it is a faithful standard counterexample with altered presentation.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
+{"id":"fs-subsequence-convergence-implies-convergence","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/ch3.pdf","https://www.math.cuhk.edu.hk/course_builder/2324/math2050b/MATH%202050%20L10%20-%20Subsequences%20and%20Bolzano-Weierstrass%20Theorem.pdf","https://www.math.cmu.edu/~gautam/c/2026-269/notes/compactness.html"],"rationale":"The sources state the one-way inheritance theorem, and Hunter's alternating sequence has constant even and odd subsequences but does not converge. The item turns that standard witness into the explicit false converse and appends the corrected every-subsequence iff, so its Statement and proof are adapted rather than exact transcriptions.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}

