+{"id":"def-divergence-to-infinity","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/ch3.pdf","https://www.nku.edu/~longa/classes/2011fall/mat420/days/highlights/highlights2.1.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter Definition 3.12 and the NKU notes give the same quantified definition of divergence to positive and negative infinity and explicitly distinguish it from convergence. The library uses 0-based indices, a weak threshold k>=K instead of n>N, and adds unboundedness/convergence consequences, so the construction is semantically identical but not textually exact.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
+{"id":"lem-reciprocal-of-null-diverges","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.nku.edu/~longa/classes/2011fall/mat420/days/highlights/highlights2.1.pdf","https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/ch3.pdf"],"rationale":"The NKU notes place the positive null sequence 1/n and its reciprocal n side by side under the definitions of convergence to zero and divergence to infinity; Hunter gives the same definitions and examples. The item abstracts that standard reciprocal bridge into an iff for arbitrary positive sequences. The two-direction epsilon/M proof is locally composed.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
