+{"id":"lem-subsequence-inherits-limit","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/ch3.pdf","https://www.math.cuhk.edu.hk/course_builder/2324/math2050b/MATH%202050%20L10%20-%20Subsequences%20and%20Bolzano-Weierstrass%20Theorem.pdf","https://www.math.cmu.edu/~gautam/c/2026-269/notes/compactness.html"],"rationale":"Hunter Proposition 3.50, the CUHK theorem, and CMU Proposition 4 state that every subsequence of a convergent sequence has the same limit; Hunter's following corollary supplies the distinct-subsequential-limits divergence test. The item combines both standard propositions and uses the 0-based n_k>=k formulation.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
+{"id":"rem-sequence-conventions","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/ch3.pdf","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Limit_of_a_sequence","https://www.math.cmu.edu/~gautam/c/2026-269/notes/compactness.html"],"rationale":"The sources collectively support the standard indexing, tail invariance, eventuality, uniqueness-before-limit-notation, bounded-range, divergence, rational/real epsilon, and subsequence conventions. The item is a library-specific synthesis with reading-order and dependency-routing commentary, not a novel mathematical claim.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
