+{"id":"fs-limit-preserves-strict-inequality","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/ch3.pdf","https://www.nku.edu/~longa/classes/2011fall/mat420/days/highlights/highlights2.1.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter's order theorem preserves only non-strict inequalities and its standard 1/n example converges through positive terms to zero; the NKU notes give the same 1/n limit and non-strict order theorem. The item packages these as the explicit false reversal and supplies an Archimedean epsilon proof, so it is a faithful standard counterexample with altered presentation.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
